[h1]0.10 Land and Larder[/h1]
For EU5 1.4.5, with or without the Across the Pillars DLC. This is a major update and not save compatible: please start a new campaign. To finish a running campaign, older versions are on [url=https://github.com/JanB1989/prosper-or-perish-constructor/releases]GitHub[/url]. The in-game Europedia explains every system.

[h2]Food[/h2]
[list]
[*] The food market is gone. Every province lives off its own store of Province Food, and the store decides whether its farms feed the province or sell to the market.
[*] Growth follows the months of food in store: an empty store slowly shrinks a province, a full two-year store grows it fastest. Reaching two years takes Granaries.
[*] Victuals are packed rations, the only food traded between provinces. They become food again where a Tavern serves them.
[*] New food buildings: the Tavern in every province capital, the Cookshop and its upgrade Public Kitchen, and the Grange. The Victualling Yard now packs staples bought on the market, and the Granary holds much more at province capitals. Cookery, Victuals Market and Food Revenue are gone.
[*] Staple foods feed nobody on the market until a farm or a Cookshop turns them into Province Food. Every staple farm cooks part of its own crop for its province.
[*] Food needs retuned: every pop type but the peasants eats less than in 0.9, prosperity raises everyone's consumption, and tribesmen live off the land.
[*] Each new age brings food relief, and the food advances slow spoilage.
[/list]

[h2]Arable Land[/h2]
[list]
[*] Population capacity is now Arable Land: the people a location's land can feed, from its climate, terrain, vegetation, soil, fertility and water.
[*] Townsfolk bring their own land. Idle peasants live off the land, and every farm level takes some.
[*] New and reworked land improvements win land: Land Clearance, Drainage Ditches, Irrigated Fields, Qanats, Paddy Water Control, Terraces, Polders and more.
[*] Development and buildings no longer add capacity, and percentage capacity bonuses are gone.
[*] The location view shows two bars, Land Usage against Land Capacity.
[/list]

[h2]The World[/h2]
[list]
[*] New geography across the map: climates, vegetation, topographies, soils and fertility, each with its own effects. What a location produces well follows them.
[*] Navigable Rivers: fleets sail the great rivers far inland, and their banks hold ports and shipyards.
[*] Rivers are redrawn on the vanilla river lines, with the gaps closed.
[*] Hostile armies move more slowly through mountains, jungles, swamps and forests.
[*] Variable Harvests swing a third as hard as before and spare herds, flocks, fish and game. You can switch them off in the game rules at any time.
[/list]

[h2]Buildings and Production[/h2]
[list]
[*] Eight crop farms with four tiers each replace the Farming Village line: Wheat, Rice, Millet, Maize, Potato, Legume, Olive and Cattle.
[*] Manual Labor is a new good: idle peasants supply it to their market, and every production method pays part of its cost in it. New Porters' Hall, Hiring Fair and Slave Gang Barracks for scarce hands.
[*] Manufacturing tiers, town rights to specialise in one good (a Charter, then Rights), and Old Ways: an upgraded workshop keeps the recipes of the buildings it replaced.
[*] RGO output is halved, except for colonial nations. Cash-crop farms produce much more per level.
[*] Upgrading a building costs less than building it new, buildings hire and lay off workers faster, and buildings without goods output can no longer be closed.
[*] The road maintenance buildings are removed.
[/list]

[h2]Markets and Logistics[/h2]
[list]
[*] Building past a location's supported levels lowers its market access; logistics buildings win it back.
[*] New regional carriers: Caravanserai, Oasis Caravan Station, Yam Station, Banjara Tanda and Llama Caravan Post.
[*] Rivers and seas carry market access much further than land.
[*] Exports and imports count fully in prices, so a shortage draws goods from where they are made. Finished crafts and staples are cheaper to ship.
[*] Colonial nations become raw-material economies.
[/list]

[h2]AI, War and Peace[/h2]
[list]
[*] The AI builds where goods are dear or short and where a province runs out of food.
[*] Peace deals take only connected land, so far less border gore, and AI nations let go of land they can never govern.
[*] The AI plans more wars. Only colonial subjects, large realms and real sea powers start colonial charters.
[*] Sea Raid casus belli for nations that may hire privateers, with a Coastal Raid toggle for fleets.
[*] Ship upkeep is the value of the ship's goods. Fleets sail faster and blockade more; sieges of unfortified places take longer.
[*] Historical aims for several AI nations, among them England, France, Castile, Portugal, Muscovy and the Ottomans.
[/list]

[h2]Interface[/h2]
[list]
[*] A rewritten Europedia with illustrated cards; every icon links to the real game object.
[*] Location view: chips for stored food, land, harvest, soil and fertility, and the two land bars.
[*] Build panel with a payback column and a payback sort order.
[*] New map modes: Navigable Rivers, Fertility, Soil Type, Victuals Price, Manual Labor Price, Province Food Growth and population change since the start.
[*] Other game languages show the English text instead of raw keys.
[/list]

Thank you to everyone who tested and gave feedback. Report bugs on Discord: [url=https://discord.gg/HgFd69Sv83]discord.gg/HgFd69Sv83[/url]
